subproblems.statement:
For every P ∈ ℕ and every real σ > 0 there is a function ρ : ℝ^P → ℝ with all of the following properties:
- ρ is C^∞;
- ρ has compact support;
- ρ(y) ≥ 0 for every y;
- ρ(y) = 0 for every y with |y| ≥ σ;
- ∫_{ℝ^P} ρ dλ^P = 1.
Here λ^P is Lebesgue measure (`volume` on `Eucl P`) and |·| is the Euclidean norm.

Let m, m′ ∈ ℕ, L ∈ ℝ≥0 and let F : ℝ^m → ℝ^{m′} (ℝ^p = Eucl p with the Euclidean norm) be L-Lipschitz on ℝ^m. Let σ > 0. Then there is a map F_σ : ℝ^m → ℝ^{m′} with the following properties, where B(x, s) denotes the open ball of radius s about x and v_i the i-th coordinate of v ∈ ℝ^{m′}:
(1) F_σ is C^∞, i.e. ContDiff ℝ ((⊤ : ℕ∞) : WithTop ℕ∞) F_σ;
(2) F_σ is L-Lipschitz on ℝ^m;
(3) |F_σ(x) − F(x)| ≤ Lσ for every x ∈ ℝ^m;
(4) (local bounds) for every x ∈ ℝ^m and every Λ′ ∈ ℝ≥0 such that F is Λ′-Lipschitz on B(x, 2σ): F_σ is Λ′-Lipschitz on B(x, σ), the operator norm of the Fréchet derivative satisfies ‖fderiv ℝ F_σ x‖ ≤ Λ′, and |F_σ(x) − F(x)| ≤ Λ′σ;
(5) (locality of coordinates) for every x ∈ ℝ^m, i ∈ Fin m′ and c ∈ ℝ, if F(y)_i = c for every y ∈ B(x, σ), then F_σ(x)_i = c.
